-- T-FIELD-VARIANT-CONFLICT (#1455 adversarial review, finding 1).
--
-- `Wrap` has TWO named-field constructors and they place `alpha` at DIFFERENT
-- offsets (0 in `Cfg`, 1 in `Dfg`).  A `.alpha` selection is resolved by the
-- receiver's TYPE, which does not say which constructor built the value, while the
-- emitted code is a single constant-offset load — so no constructor key can be
-- stamped and the access is refused.
--
-- WHAT THIS PINS IS A REJECT THAT REPLACES A SILENTLY WRONG ACCEPT.  MEASURED on a
-- cold build of the merge base `bfcd4ea7`, at exit 0 with no diagnostic: `medaka
-- run` printed the correct `11,22` while the BUILT BINARY printed `22,11`; with the
-- two fields at different offsets AND different types the built binary SIGSEGVed
-- (exit 139).  So the golden here is not "the compiler got stricter" — it is the
-- only statically honest answer to a program whose meaning the back end cannot
-- represent.
--
-- ⚠️ THE OFFSETS ARE THE RULE, NOT A DETAIL OF THE MESSAGE — AND THE CONTROLS ARE
-- WHERE THAT IS PINNED.  A sibling constructor that declares `alpha` at the SAME
-- position is not a rival (`field_variant_offset_agree_control.mdk`), and a sibling
-- that declares a different field entirely is not one either
-- (`field_variant_sibling_other_field_control.mdk`).  Round 3 of #1455 briefly
-- dropped the offset test and rejected both of those shapes; they were measured
-- CORRECT on both shipping backends, so it was over-rejection and it was retracted.
-- `field_variant_single_ctor_control.mdk` is the third control: one named-field
-- constructor per type, so the stamp IS the runtime constructor.
--
-- ⚠️ THE REFUSED SET IS NOT THE SET THE BACKENDS DISAGREE ABOUT, AND NOTHING HERE
-- CLAIMS IT IS.  The guard is a declaration-level over-approximation of a runtime
-- dataflow property; #1469 (WasmGC traps where two constructors reach an
-- offset-AGREEING access) and #1468 (a sibling declaring a different field) are
-- both accepted here and both broken.  #1465 owns the end state that retires this
-- diagnostic.
data Wrap =
  | Cfg { alpha : Int, beta : Int }
  | Dfg { beta : Int, alpha : Int }

getAlpha : Wrap -> Int
getAlpha w = w.alpha
